<p>I got a bigger and better monitor recently, and I&rsquo;ve been exploring calibrating it on both my Mac and my Linux desktop for the last week or so.</p>
<p>It started out as a necessity: at full resolution (3840 x 2160), the text was just too small for me to read. Since I hated the fact that things looked &ldquo;off&rdquo; at a lower resolution, and the options for my new external display on the Mac were painfully minimal, I quickly found out about <a href="https://betterdisplay.pro/">BetterDisplay</a>.</p>
<p>A couple of days later I was already reading about the differences between Retina and HiDPI (mostly a marketing term by Apple, though Retina does specify a higher number of HiDPI, High Dots Per Inch, which hey, should be PPI, Pixels Per Inch, but that&rsquo;s another thing), how to sync brightness between my monitors, found out that TrueTone is a whole Apple thing (but Night Shift isn&rsquo;t, mostly anyway) and wrote myself notes on how to set things up. I don&rsquo;t know how I found the time while still researching my recent <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/08/05/emacs-config-gems-part.html">Emacs stuff recently</a>, which I&rsquo;m still very much into at the moment. All I can say is that 5 hours of sleep a night have <em>some</em> benefits (don&rsquo;t do that. I don&rsquo;t recommend).</p>
<p>This morning I started looking into a similar degree of power in Linux. My Desktop is mostly used for Gaming (in terms of graphics anyway). The new screen gave me that &ldquo;buttery smooth&rdquo; feeling in games with the new refresh rate, which I didn&rsquo;t know I was missing on the old display. The new resolution is awesome, but the real big benefit is the size of the new monitor (it&rsquo;s a 32-inch), and a better aspect ratio (16:9, vs my Ultra-wide, which is 21:9), which means I get to see more without moving the mouse, and things are way more immersive (I now go for solo virtuals &ldquo;walks&rdquo; in Helldivers to relax and enjoy the view, which is not what you&rsquo;re supposed to do in Helldivers, but so what).</p>
<p>On Linux, Kubuntu 24.04 LTS in my case, I still use X11, which doesn&rsquo;t even come with a built-in brightness bar. I could play with Dell&rsquo;s firmware to get something working quickly, but BetterDisplay spoiled me. Because <a href="https://kubuntu.org/news/">Kubuntu 26.04 LTS</a> is now out, I think it&rsquo;s time for me to wipe and reinstall things on the Desktop yet again (last time was when <a href="https://taonaw.com/2025/11/24/kubuntu-instead-of-pop-os.html">I switched from PopOS</a>). The built-in Wayland (which will come with more options) is just one more thing I want to try, but really, my Linux Desktop is a messy collection of various libraries and programs I downloaded and set up and forgot about, and I need to do it all over again slowly with the right documentation.</p>
<p>I think I need another full week&rsquo;s vacation to kick this one off, though, if we&rsquo;re talking the kind of research and documentation I left for myself in terms of my Emacs config, but I think it will be worth it.</p>
